TITLE: Fix tests with /coder

# Fix tests with /coder

In this recipe, you will see how the AI calls engineering tools (`@tool_call`) to fix tests.

Start `/coder` mode with a clear instruction about what needs to be fixed.

```bash
/coder fix the failing tests
```

The AI analyzes the request and calls the plugin to run the tests and identify the errors:

```xml
<plan>
Run go test to see errors.
</plan>
<tool_call name="@coder" args="{&quot;cmd&quot;:&quot;test&quot;,&quot;args&quot;:{&quot;dir&quot;:&quot;.&quot;}}"/>
```

The command result is returned to the AI. ChatCLI acts as an intermediary, executing the requested command and capturing the output.

Based on the errors found, the AI requests to read the problematic file:

```xml
<plan>
Read the file with the error.
</plan>
<tool_call name="@coder" args="{&quot;cmd&quot;:&quot;read&quot;,&quot;args&quot;:{&quot;file&quot;:&quot;main.go&quot;}}"/>
```

After analyzing the code, the AI generates and applies the necessary patch:

```xml
<plan>
Apply patch to fix logic.
</plan>
<tool_call name="@coder" args="{&quot;cmd&quot;:&quot;patch&quot;,&quot;args&quot;:{&quot;file&quot;:&quot;main.go&quot;,&quot;encoding&quot;:&quot;base64&quot;,&quot;search&quot;:&quot;base64_old&quot;,&quot;replace&quot;:&quot;base64_new&quot;}}"/>
```

This entire flow happens autonomously within `/coder` mode. The AI executes a ReAct loop (Reason + Act) where each step informs the next, until all tests pass.



===== FILE: cookbook/cross-layer-knowledge-debug.mdx =====
TITLE: Recipe: Cross-Layer Debugging with 3 Knowledge Bases

# Recipe: Cross-Layer Debugging with 3 Knowledge Bases

An incident rarely lives in one layer. The pod won't go up — is it the **Argo manifest**? the **Terraform node group**? the **health check in code**? This recipe indexes all three layers as separate [knowledge bases](/features/knowledge-base) with [`@docs-flatten kind=code`](/features/knowledge-base) and lets the agent **cross** the bases to find the root cause.

## The Problem

The `checkout-api` `Rollout` is stuck in `Progressing` and never turns `Healthy`. Looking one layer at a time gets nowhere: the manifest looks right, Terraform applied without error, and the code compiles. The answer is **at the seam** — and it's spread across three repositories.

## Ingredients

-   The application repository (service code).
-   The infra Terraform module (cluster, node groups).
-   The GitOps repository (Argo/Kubernetes manifests).
-   ChatCLI with the builtin `@docs-flatten` tool (included).

No API key required: knowledge mode uses pure-Go BM25 by default. Embeddings ([Voyage/OpenAI/Bedrock](/features/quality/rag-hyde)) are an optional boost.

---

## Step by Step

`kind=code` slices each repo by structure — functions, Terraform resources, manifests — with symbol/resource titles, instead of one text blob:

```bash
@docs-flatten root=./checkout-api  kind=code format=jsonl output=app.jsonl
@docs-flatten root=./infra-eks     kind=code format=jsonl output=infra.jsonl
@docs-flatten root=./gitops-argo   kind=code format=jsonl output=argo.jsonl
```

Noise (`vendor/`, `.terraform/`, lockfiles, binaries) is skipped automatically, and files above 1 MiB are ignored.

```bash
/context create app   app.jsonl   --mode knowledge
/context create infra infra.jsonl --mode knowledge
/context create argo  argo.jsonl  --mode knowledge

/context attach app && /context attach infra && /context attach argo
```

Each attach costs only an **index card** (~900 tokens, fixed) in the prompt — all three together stay cheap, even with large repos.

In `/agent` (or `/coder`), describe the symptom and let the AI connect. `@knowledge search` **fans out across the three bases**, tagging each passage by its source base:

```
/agent the checkout-api Rollout stays in Progressing and never goes Healthy.
       connect the Argo manifest, the Terraform node group and the
       readiness/health check in the service code.
```

Under the hood, the agent investigates iteratively:

```
→ @knowledge search "checkout-api Rollout readiness"      (base: argo)
→ @knowledge search "node group taints capacity"          (base: infra)
→ @knowledge search "health readiness endpoint port"      (base: app)
→ @knowledge get "argo/rollout-checkout.yaml"
```

With passages from all three layers in the same reasoning, the AI connects the dots — for example:

> The `Rollout/checkout-api` (argo) sets `readinessProbe` on port **8080**, but the service (`app`, `server.go`) listens on **8081**; and `aws_eks_node_group.workers` (infra) has a `dedicated=checkout` taint the `Rollout` doesn't tolerate. **Two compounding factors**: the probe never passes and the pods don't even schedule onto the right nodes.

The base is **read-only** — it gives understanding. To **apply the fix** and run the tests, use `/coder` on the live repo with `@read`/`@search`/`@coder`.

---

## Why it works

    `@knowledge search` queries **all** attached bases at once; each hit is tagged by its source base, so the model knows which layer it came from.
  
    Terraform resources, K8s manifests and functions become their own chunks with titles (`aws_eks_node_group.workers`, `Rollout/checkout-api`) — search lands on the right spot.
  
    A ~900-token index card per base, regardless of repo size. Three layers still fit in the prompt.
  
    Pure-Go BM25 covers everything with no API key; identifiers (`getReadiness`, `checkout_api`) are found by their parts thanks to camelCase/snake splitting.
  
You don't have to say "this is code" for each repo: the agent picks `kind=code` on its own from intent and a self-correcting hint. See [Knowledge Base](/features/knowledge-base).

## Next steps



===== FILE: cookbook/debugging-logs-with-pipes.mdx =====
TITLE: Recipe: Debugging Error Logs with Pipes

# Recipe: Debugging Error Logs with Pipes

Analyzing lengthy log files can be a time-consuming and frustrating task. This recipe will show you how to use **ChatCLI** together with `cat`, `grep`, or `kubectl logs` to turn the AI into your personal log analyst.

## The Problem

You are facing a bug in production. The log file has thousands of lines with `INFO`, `WARN`, and `ERROR`. Finding the relevant exception and understanding the cascade of events that led to the error is like finding a needle in a haystack.

## Ingredients

-   Access to a log file (e.g., `app.log`, `/var/log/syslog`).
-   Or access to a command that generates logs (e.g., `kubectl logs <pod-name>`).

---

## Step by Step

The magic happens when you use the pipe operator (`|`) from your shell to send the output of a command directly to `chatcli` in one-shot mode (`-p`).

Analyze a complete log file by asking the AI to identify the most frequent error and explain its cause.

```bash
cat /var/log/nginx/error.log | chatcli -p "Analyze this Nginx log, identify the most recurring error, explain the likely cause, and suggest a command to fix it."
```

If you are a DevOps engineer, you can do this directly with `kubectl`.

```bash
kubectl logs my-app-pod-xyz-123 | chatcli -p "This is the log of a pod that is restarting. Find the fatal exception (panic) and tell me which source code file and line it likely occurred at."
```

If the log is massive, sending everything can be inefficient. You can pre-filter the relevant lines with `grep` and send only the error context to the AI.

Let's assume you know the error is related to a `NullPointerException`.

```bash
grep -C 20 "NullPointerException" application.log | chatcli -p "The main error is a NullPointerException. Based on the 20 lines of context before and after, what is the call sequence that led to this error?"
```

**Deconstructing the Command:**
- `grep -C 20 "NullPointerException"`: Finds the line with the error and captures **20 lines of context** (`C`) before and after.
- `| chatcli -p "..."`: Sends this focused excerpt to the AI with a very specific question, resulting in a faster and more accurate analysis.

The AI will return a structured analysis, saving you time on reading and interpretation.

**Example AI Response (for the Kubernetes scenario):**

```markdown
The log analysis indicates a fatal exception (panic) caused by a null pointer.

**Likely Root Cause:**
The exception `panic: runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference` occurs in the following call stack:
- `main.go:152`: in the `processPayment()` function
- `main.go:98`: in the `handleRequest()` function

This suggests that the `dbConnection` variable in the `processPayment` function was nil when called.

**Suggested Fix:**
Verify that the database connection was properly initialized before calling `processPayment()` at line 98 of `main.go`. Add a nil check:

`if dbConnection == nil { return fmt.Errorf("database connection is not active") }`
```

**Result:** Instead of spending minutes (or hours) reading logs, you got the root cause, the location in the code, and a fix suggestion in seconds.



===== FILE: cookbook/k8s-monitoring.mdx =====
TITLE: Recipe: K8s Monitoring with AI

# Recipe: K8s Monitoring with AI

This recipe puts the [K8s Watcher](/features/k8s-watcher) to work on an application `myapp` in the namespace `production`, in three setups:

| Option | Runs | Good for |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Local | `chatcli watch` on your machine, with your kubeconfig | one engineer, ad-hoc diagnosis |
| 2. Team server | `chatcli server` with a watcher, deployed with Helm | a team sharing one context and one set of API keys |
| 3. AIOps | an operator-managed `Instance` | alerts turned into Issues, AI analysis and remediation |

## Option 1: local monitoring

    ```bash
    kubectl config current-context
    kubectl get deploy myapp -n production
    kubectl auth can-i list pods -n production
    kubectl auth can-i get pods/log -n production
    kubectl auth can-i list events -n production
    ```

    The watcher uses `~/.kube/config` with its current context (or `--kubeconfig`); it does not read `KUBECONFIG`.
  
    ```bash
    chatcli watch --deployment myapp --namespace production
    ```

    ```text
    Starting K8s watcher: 1 targets (interval: 30s, window: 2h0m0s)
    Initial data collected. K8s context will be injected into all prompts.
    Type your questions about the deployments. K8s context is automatically included.
    Use /watch to see current status.
    ```
  
    ```text
    > Is the deployment healthy?
    > Why is pod myapp-7d9f-abcde restarting?
    > What warning events happened in the last 30 minutes?
    > Show the most recent error logs and what they have in common
    ```

    Every prompt carries the deployment status, pods, events, recent error logs, HPA, node health and active alerts. `/watch status` prints the one-line state.
  
### Tune it

| Flag | Default | When to change |
|---|---|---|
| `--interval` | `30s` | `10s` while you actively debug; `60s` for a quiet environment |
| `--window` | `2h` | `30m` for a quick look; `6h` or more to compare with earlier |
| `--max-log-lines` | `100` | lower for chatty apps; higher for deep debugging |

```bash
chatcli watch --deployment myapp --namespace production --interval 10s --window 6h --max-log-lines 300
```

### Several workloads, other kinds, Prometheus metrics

```yaml
# targets.yaml
interval: "15s"
window: "2h"
maxContextChars: 32000
targets:
  - deployment: frontend
    namespace: production
    metricsPort: 3000
    metricsFilter: ["http_*"]
  - deployment: backend
    namespace: production
    metricsPort: 9090
    metricsFilter: ["http_requests_*", "db_*", "cache_*"]
  - deployment: postgres
    kind: StatefulSet
    namespace: production
  - deployment: nightly-report
    kind: CronJob
    namespace: batch
```

```bash
chatcli watch --config targets.yaml
```

Targets with problems get full detail, healthy ones a single line, within `maxContextChars`. Prometheus scraping reads pod IPs over plain HTTP, so from a laptop it only works if your network routes to pod IPs; otherwise the other data still flows and the metrics section is simply absent.

### One-shot for alert handlers

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# alert-handler.sh <deployment> <namespace>
set -euo pipefail
ANALYSIS=$(chatcli watch --deployment "$1" --namespace "$2" \
  -p "Analyze the current state of this deployment and identify the most likely root cause. Answer in Markdown.")

jq -n --arg text "*ChatCLI analysis of $2/$1*"$'\n\n'"$ANALYSIS" '{text: $text}' \
  | curl -sS -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' --data @- "$SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL"
```

Each run collects once (up to 10 seconds) before asking, so the answer reflects the current state.

## Option 2: team server with a watcher (Helm)

    ```bash
    helm install chatcli oci://ghcr.io/diillson/charts/chatcli \
      --version 1.211.2 \
      --namespace monitoring --create-namespace \
      --set llm.provider=CLAUDEAI \
      --set secrets.anthropicApiKey="$ANTHROPIC_API_KEY" \
      --set server.token="$(openssl rand -hex 32)" \
      --set watcher.enabled=true \
      --set watcher.deployment=myapp \
      --set watcher.namespace=production \
      --set watcher.interval=15s
    ```

    - `server.token` is required: in a pod the server listens on every interface and refuses to start without a credential.
    - The server log reaches `kubectl logs` on its own: in a pod the server also writes it to stderr as JSON lines. The file copy in the pod rotates at 20 MB with 3 backups (`logging.*`), which fits the pod's 200Mi data volume.
    - An empty `watcher.namespace` watches `default`. A namespace other than the release's (`production` here, outside `monitoring`) makes the chart create a ClusterRole automatically. For several workloads, or a StatefulSet, DaemonSet, Job or CronJob (`kind`), use `watcher.targets` in a values file (see [Helm watcher values](/getting-started/docker-deployment#k8s-watcher)).
  
    ```bash
    kubectl -n monitoring rollout status deploy/chatcli
    kubectl -n monitoring logs deploy/chatcli | grep -E 'listening|watcher active'
    # 🚀 ChatCLI server listening on 0.0.0.0:50051
    # K8s watcher active: 1 targets (interval: 15s)
    ```
  
    ```bash
    export CHATCLI_REMOTE_TOKEN="$(kubectl -n monitoring get secret chatcli \
      -o jsonpath='{.data.CHATCLI_SERVER_TOKEN}' | base64 -d)"

    kubectl -n monitoring port-forward svc/chatcli 50051:50051 &
    CHATCLI_ALLOW_INSECURE=true chatcli connect localhost:50051 --token "$CHATCLI_REMOTE_TOKEN"
    ```

    ```text
    Connected to ChatCLI server (version: 1.211.2, provider: CLAUDEAI, model: claude-sonnet-5)
    K8s watcher active: multi/1 targets (context injected into all prompts)
    ```

    The server adds the watcher context to every prompt, so each teammate just asks. `CHATCLI_ALLOW_INSECURE=true` is needed because this server is plaintext; for anything beyond a port-forward turn on [TLS](/getting-started/docker-deployment#tls) and connect with `--tls --ca-cert`.
  
Distribute access with one token per person by switching to JWTs (`security.jwtSecretRef`), so each caller has its own identity and rate-limit bucket; see [Server authentication](/features/server-mode#server-authentication).

### Incident workflow

```bash
chatcli connect localhost:50051 --token "$CHATCLI_REMOTE_TOKEN"   # add CHATCLI_ALLOW_INSECURE=true for plaintext
```

```text
> Summarize the current state of myapp for an incident channel
> What warning events occurred in the last 30 minutes?
> Show the most recent error logs
> What is the most likely root cause, and what should I do first?
> After the fix: are the pods back to normal? Compare with 20 minutes ago.
```

The comparison works within `watcher.window` (default 2 hours). `/watch status` in this session queries the server's watcher (`K8s Watcher (remote): …`).

For scripts against the server:

```bash
CHATCLI_ALLOW_INSECURE=true chatcli connect localhost:50051 --token "$CHATCLI_REMOTE_TOKEN" \
  -p "myapp is failing. Analyze and suggest a fix." --raw
```

## Option 3: autonomous AIOps (operator)

The operator provisions the server from an `Instance`, reads the watcher's alerts over `StreamAlerts`, and drives `Anomaly` → `Issue` → `AIInsight` → `RemediationPlan`. It dials the server over **TLS** with a **credential**, so the Instance needs both.

    ```bash
    helm install chatcli-operator oci://ghcr.io/diillson/charts/chatcli-operator \
      --version 1.211.2 \
      -n chatcli-system --create-namespace
    ```
  
    Follow [Your first Instance](/features/k8s-operator#your-first-instance) for the three Secrets in the namespace `chatcli`: `chatcli-api-keys` (provider key), `chatcli-server-token` (key `token`) and `chatcli-tls` (`tls.crt`, `tls.key`, `ca.crt`, valid for `chatcli.chatcli.svc.cluster.local`).
  
    ```yaml
    apiVersion: platform.chatcli.io/v1alpha1
    kind: Instance
    metadata:
      name: chatcli
      namespace: chatcli
    spec:
      provider: CLAUDEAI
      apiKeys:
        name: chatcli-api-keys
      server:
        port: 50051
        token:
          name: chatcli-server-token
          key: token
        tls:
          enabled: true
          secretName: chatcli-tls
      watcher:
        enabled: true
        interval: "15s"
        targets:
          - deployment: api-gateway
            namespace: production
            metricsPort: 9090
          - deployment: backend
            namespace: production
          - deployment: worker
            namespace: batch
    ```

    ```bash
    kubectl apply -f instance.yaml
    kubectl -n chatcli get instance chatcli
    ```

    Without `server.token` (or another credential) the operator does not create the Deployment (`AuthenticationConfigured=False`); without TLS it cannot reach the server (`ServerReachable=False`). `namespace` is required on every target. The pipeline uses the first ready Instance in the cluster.
  
    ```bash
    kubectl get anomalies -A --watch
    kubectl get issues -A
    kubectl get aiinsights -A
    kubectl get remediationplans -A
    kubectl get runbooks -A
    kubectl get postmortems -A
    ```

    A crash-looping pod raises `HighRestartCount` (and `OOMKilled` when that is the cause); the operator records an `Anomaly`, correlates it into an `Issue`, asks the server for an analysis (`AnalyzeIssue`), and remediates from a matching Runbook, AI-suggested actions, or an agentic observe-decide-act loop (`AgenticStep`, up to `spec.aiops.agenticMaxSteps`, default 10). The full flow, approvals and safety limits are in [AIOps Platform](/features/aiops-platform).
  
    ```yaml
    apiVersion: platform.chatcli.io/v1alpha1
    kind: Runbook
    metadata:
      name: oom-standard-procedure
      namespace: production
    spec:
      description: "Standard OOMKill recovery for production"
      trigger:
        signalType: oom_kill
        severity: critical
        resourceKind: Deployment
      steps:
        - name: Restart pods
          action: RestartDeployment
          description: "Restart to reclaim leaked memory"
        - name: Scale up
          action: ScaleDeployment
          description: "Add replicas for redundancy"
          params:
            replicas: "5"
      maxAttempts: 2
    ```

    A manual Runbook that matches the Issue is used before AI-generated actions.
  
## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `failed to create K8s watcher: …` | No usable kubeconfig or cluster unreachable | Check `kubectl get pods`; pass `--kubeconfig` |
| Context has no pods or events, log shows `forbidden` | Missing RBAC | Grant the [watcher rules](/features/k8s-watcher#rbac) |
| Server pod crash-loops; `kubectl logs` shows `refusing to serve an unauthenticated API` | No credential | Set `server.token` |
| `tls: first record does not look like a TLS handshake` on connect | Plaintext server | `CHATCLI_ALLOW_INSECURE=true`, or enable TLS |
| `/watch status` after `chatcli connect` says no watcher | The server runs none: no `K8s watcher active` line at connect | Set `watcher.enabled=true` and a target on the server chart |
| No `## Application Metrics` | Pod IPs unreachable, wrong `metricsPort`/`metricsPath`, or filter matches nothing | Run the watcher in the cluster; check port and filter |
| Instance never gets a Deployment | `AuthenticationConfigured=False` or `TLSConfigured=False` | Add `server.token` and `tls.secretName` |
| No Anomalies appear | `ServerReachable=False`, or no alert raised yet | Check the Instance conditions; see [K8s Operator troubleshooting](/features/k8s-operator#troubleshooting) |

## Checklist

    - [ ] `kubectl` access and RBAC for pods, pods/log, events, workloads
    - [ ] Local (`chatcli watch`) or shared (`chatcli server` / Helm `watcher.*`)
    - [ ] Single target (`--deployment`) or `targets.yaml` (`--config` / `--watch-config`)
    - [ ] Optional `metricsPort` and `metricsFilter` for Prometheus scraping
    - [ ] Interval and window fit the scenario; `maxContextChars` for many targets
    - [ ] Server: credential set, TLS before exposing it
    - [ ] Test: "Is the deployment healthy?"
  
    - [ ] Operator installed with `--version 1.211.2`
    - [ ] Secrets: provider key, server token, TLS (`tls.crt`, `tls.key`, `ca.crt`)
    - [ ] Instance with `server.token`, `server.tls`, `watcher.enabled` and targets with `namespace`
    - [ ] Conditions all `True`, `ServerReachable=True`
    - [ ] `kubectl get anomalies,issues -A` fill in when something fails
    - [ ] Optional Runbooks for known scenarios
  


===== FILE: cookbook/multi-project-workflow.mdx =====
TITLE: Recipe: Managing Multiple Projects with Contexts and Sessions

# Recipe: Managing Multiple Projects with Contexts and Sessions

Switching between projects can break your workflow, especially when each one has its own codebase and issue history. This recipe shows how to use **Persistent Contexts** and **Session Management** to switch between projects instantly and in an organized way.

## The Problem

You work on two projects: `Project A` (a Go API) and `Project B` (a React frontend). One moment you are debugging a bug in the API; the next, you need to implement a new feature in the frontend. Keeping the conversation history and file context separate for each is a challenge.

## Ingredients

-   Two or more project directories on your machine.

---

## The Workflow

The strategy is to create a **Context** for each project's codebase and a **Session** for each task's conversation history.

First, let's create "snapshots" of the codebases. We will do this once for each project.

Navigate to the `Project A` folder:

```bash
/context create project-a-api ./cmd ./internal --mode smart --description "Main Go API for Project A" --tags go,api
```

Now, navigate to the `Project B` folder:

```bash
/context create project-b-frontend ./src --mode chunked --description "React frontend for Project B" --tags react,frontend
```

**What did we do?** We created two persistent contexts. Now, ChatCLI "knows" the structure of these two projects and we can refer to them by name (`project-a-api`, `project-b-frontend`) at any time, from anywhere on your system.

Let's begin debugging the bug in the API.

1.  **Load (or create) a conversation session:**

    ```bash
    /session load debug-api-project-a
    ```

    *(If the session does not exist, it will be created when you save.)*

2.  **Attach the project context:**

    ```bash
    /context attach project-a-api
    ```

Now, your prompt will indicate that the session and context are active. You can start asking questions about the API code, and ChatCLI will automatically send the relevant files to the AI.

```text
debug-api-project-a > Where is the JWT validation logic?
```

Your lead asks you to pause the debug and work on a new feature in the frontend.

1.  **Save the current session:** All your debug history will be preserved.

    ```bash
    /session save debug-api-project-a
    ```

2.  **Detach the old context:**

    ```bash
    /context detach project-a-api
    ```

3.  **Load the session and context for Project B:**

    ```bash
    /session load feature-frontend-project-b
    /context attach project-b-frontend
    ```

Done! In seconds, you have completely switched environments. Your conversation history is now about the frontend feature, and the AI has all of `Project B`'s code as context.

```text
feature-frontend-project-b > Create a new React component called 'UserProfile' that displays name and email.
```

Frontend task completed? Going back to the API debug is just as simple.

```bash
/session save feature-frontend-project-b
/context detach project-b-frontend
/session load debug-api-project-a
/context attach project-a-api
```

**Result:** You created a workflow where you can switch between complex projects in seconds, preserving both your conversation history and the complete codebase context, without ever needing to resend a single file manually.



===== FILE: cookbook/onboarding-new-project.mdx =====
TITLE: Recipe: Understanding a Code Project

# Recipe: Understanding a Code Project

Joining a new project can be challenging. Where are the main files? What is the architecture? How does the authentication flow work? This recipe shows how to use **ChatCLI** to drastically accelerate your onboarding process.

## The Problem

You cloned a repository with hundreds of files and need to become productive as quickly as possible. Reading everything is impractical, and the documentation may be outdated.

## Ingredients

-   A code repository cloned to your local machine.

---

## Step by Step

This workflow uses a "top-down" approach: first we get an architecture overview and then we dive into specific details.

The first step is to get a map of the territory. The `summary` mode is perfect for this, as it lists the file and directory structure without sending the code content.

```bash
@file --mode=summary . > Describe the overall architecture of this project based on the file structure. Which components appear to be the most important?
```

**What to expect from the AI:**

The AI will return a high-level analysis, such as:

```text
This appears to be a Go backend project.
The /cmd directory suggests there are multiple binaries.

The /internal/api folder likely contains the HTTP route handlers,
while /internal/database manages the database access logic.

The docker-compose.yml file indicates the project uses Docker for
the development environment.
```

With this, you already have a mental map of where to look for things.

Now that you have an overview, you can ask specific questions. The `smart` mode is ideal here, because you don't need to know which files contain the answer. The AI will find them for you.

Let's investigate the authentication flow.

```bash
@file --mode=smart ./internal/ > Explain the end-to-end authentication flow, from the API route to token validation.
```

**How it works:**
1. ChatCLI sends the list of all files inside `./internal/` to the AI.
2. The AI, based on the question "authentication flow," identifies that files like `auth_handler.go`, `jwt_service.go`, and `user_repository.go` are likely the most relevant.
3. ChatCLI then sends the content of **only those selected files**.
4. The AI answers your question based on the actual code from the most important files.

During your investigation, you may want to understand a specific file in detail. Now, use the `full` mode (or simply omit the mode, as it is the default).

```bash
@file ./internal/api/middleware/auth.go > Refactor this middleware to follow Go best practices, adding more robust error handling and logging.
```

The AI now has the complete file content and can perform complex tasks such as refactoring, documentation, or test generation.

**Result:** In less than 15 minutes, you went from zero knowledge of the project to:
1. Understanding the high-level architecture.
2. Mapping a critical business flow (authentication).
3. Analyzing and even refactoring a specific code component.

This workflow transforms hours or days of manual exploration into a focused and efficient question-and-answer session.



===== FILE: cookbook/oneshot-pipelines.mdx =====
TITLE: One-Shot, Pipes, and Automation

# One-Shot, Pipes, and Automation

ChatCLI's one-shot mode allows you to run prompts in a single line, ideal for scripts, CI/CD pipelines, and automations.

## Basic One-Shot Mode

Use the `-p` flag to send a prompt and exit immediately:

```bash
chatcli -p "Briefly explain this repository."
```

All context features work within the prompt:

```bash
chatcli -p "@git @env Create a concise release note."
chatcli -p "@file ./src --mode summary Provide an architecture overview."
```

---

## Pipes and stdin

ChatCLI accepts input via pipe, combining the output of other commands with your prompt:

```bash
# Analyze Git diff
git diff | chatcli -p "Summarize the changes and list possible impacts."

# Diagnose errors
cat error.log | chatcli -p "Explain the root cause and suggest a solution."

# Analyze command output
kubectl get pods -n production | chatcli -p "Which pods are having issues?"

# Review code
cat src/auth.go | chatcli -p "Review this code and point out vulnerabilities."
```

---

## Available Flags

| Flag | Description | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `-p`, `--prompt` | Text to send to the LLM | `-p "Explain this"` |
| `--provider` | Override the AI provider | `--provider CLAUDEAI` |
| `--model` | Override the model | `--model gpt-4o` |
| `--max-tokens` | Token limit for the response | `--max-tokens 1000` |
| `--timeout` | Call timeout (Go duration) | `--timeout 30s` |
| `--no-anim` | Disable animations (useful in scripts) | `--no-anim` |
| `--agent-auto-exec` | Automatically execute the first suggested command | `--agent-auto-exec` |
| `--realm` | Realm/tenant for StackSpot | `--realm my-tenant` |
| `--agent-id` | Agent ID for StackSpot | `--agent-id xxx` |

---

## One-Shot Agent Mode

Combine `/agent` in the prompt to delegate tasks:

```bash
# Dry-run: only suggests the command and exits
chatcli -p "/agent list all .go files in this directory"

# Automatic execution: runs the first safe command
chatcli -p "/agent create a file called test_file.txt" --agent-auto-exec
```

With `--agent-auto-exec`, dangerous commands are automatically blocked. Only commands considered safe will be executed.

---

## Automation Examples

### Generate release notes automatically

```bash
#!/bin/bash
chatcli -p "@git Create detailed release notes for the latest tag." \
  --provider CLAUDEAI \
  --no-anim > RELEASE_NOTES.md
```

### Review PR in CI

```bash
#!/bin/bash
git diff main...HEAD | chatcli -p "Review this diff. List security issues, potential bugs, and suggestions." \
  --no-anim \
  --max-tokens 2000
```

### Analyze logs in a cron job

```bash
#!/bin/bash
tail -100 /var/log/nginx/error.log | chatcli -p "Summarize the errors and suggest corrective actions." \
  --no-anim >> /var/log/ai-analysis.log
```

### One-shot with a specific provider

```bash
chatcli -p "@file ./src --mode summary Describe the architecture" \
  --provider STACKSPOT \
  --agent-id "your-agent-id"
```

---

## Tips

Create an alias in your shell for quick access: `alias c='chatcli'` and `alias ai='chatcli -p'`. Then use: `ai "Explain what this command does: iptables -t nat -L"`

- Use `--no-anim` in **all** scripts to avoid escape characters in the output
- Combine with `>` to redirect output to files
- Use `--timeout` to prevent scripts from hanging while waiting for a response
- Double quotes are important: `chatcli -p "text with @file and @git"` (protects against shell interpretation)



===== FILE: cookbook/release-notes-automation.mdx =====
TITLE: Recipe: Generating Release Notes with @git

# Recipe: Generating Release Notes with @git

Keeping a `CHANGELOG.md` up to date is crucial, but it can be a tedious task. This recipe will show you how to use ChatCLI's `@git` command to analyze your recent changes and generate a release notes proposal in seconds.

## The Problem

After completing a series of commits for a new version, you need to summarize what was done. The manual process usually involves:
1.  Running `git log --oneline` to see the commits.
2.  Analyzing each commit message.
3.  Grouping changes by type (New Features, Bug Fixes, etc.).
4.  Writing a summary in Markdown.

Let's automate this.

---

## Ingredients

For this recipe, you will need:
-   To be in the root folder of a Git repository.
-   To have some committed changes since the last tag/version.

---

## Step by Step

The `@git` command automatically collects crucial information from your repository: the current branch, file status, diffs, and most importantly, recent commits.

Let's combine this with a clear prompt, instructing the AI on the format we want.

```bash
@git > Analyze the commits and code changes and generate a release notes proposal for version v1.2.0. Group the changes into sections such as "New Features", "Bug Fixes", and "Internal Improvements". Use Markdown format.
```

**Deconstructing the Prompt:**
- **`@git >`**: The command injects all Git context. The `>` operator separates the context from our instruction.
- **"Analyze the commits and changes..."**: We ask the AI to not only read the commit messages but also correlate them with the code diffs provided.
- **"generate a release notes proposal..."**: We make the final goal clear.
- **"Group the changes into sections..."**: We give the AI a clear structure to follow, ensuring a consistent result.

ChatCLI will send the context to the AI, which will process the information and return a formatted response.

**Example AI Response:**

```markdown
Sure! Based on the commits and code changes, here is a release notes proposal for **v1.2.0**.

---

### Release Notes - v1.2.0

This release introduces the powerful persistent context management system and improves Agent Mode performance.

#### New Features

-   **Persistent Context Management (`/context`)**: It is now possible to create, save, attach, and manage project "snapshots" for reuse across different sessions. This includes the `create`, `attach`, `list`, and `delete` subcommands.
-   **Smart File Mode (`@file --mode=smart`)**: A new mode that allows the AI to select the most relevant files for the user's question, ideal for large codebases.

#### Bug Fixes

-   Fixed a bug in Agent Mode where the `p` key to toggle the view did not work correctly on Windows.
-   Resolved a concurrency issue that could cause a panic when canceling a request (`Ctrl+C`) during file processing.

#### Internal Improvements

-   Optimized file processing in the `@file` command, resulting in up to 30% faster reading in directories with many small files.
-   Refactored `LLMManager` to allow more flexible configuration of retry policies (attempts and backoff).

---
```

Now you have a high-quality draft for your release notes. Simply copy the text, paste it into your `CHANGELOG.md` or your GitHub release description, make any fine-tuning adjustments you see fit, and you are done!

You turned a 15-minute task into a single 15-second command.

---

## Next Steps

Use pipes and `@command` to analyze error logs quickly

Use `@file --mode=summary` to explore unfamiliar codebases



===== FILE: cookbook/remote-server-setup.mdx =====
TITLE: Recipe: Remote Server for Teams

# Recipe: Remote Server for Teams

In this recipe, you will set up a centralized ChatCLI server that serves the entire development team. Each member can connect from their terminal and use the AI without needing to manage individual API keys.

---

## Scenario

5-10 developers sharing resources

Central server with managed keys

Each dev connects from their local terminal

Shared token or individual credentials

---

Create a `.env` file on the server:

```bash
# .env
CHATCLI_SERVER_TOKEN=team-token-2024
LLM_PROVIDER=CLAUDEAI
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-xxx-corporate-key
ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-6
LOG_LEVEL=info
```

Start with Docker Compose:

```bash
docker compose up -d
```

```bash
export CHATCLI_SERVER_TOKEN=team-token-2024
export LLM_PROVIDER=CLAUDEAI
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-xxx
chatcli server --port 50051
```

```bash
helm install chatcli oci://ghcr.io/diillson/charts/chatcli \
  --namespace tools --create-namespace \
  --set llm.provider=CLAUDEAI \
  --set secrets.anthropicApiKey=sk-ant-xxx \
  --set server.token=team-token-2024 \
  --set service.type=LoadBalancer
```

Share with the team:

```bash
# Add to each dev's .bashrc or .zshrc
export CHATCLI_REMOTE_ADDR=ai-server:50051
export CHATCLI_REMOTE_TOKEN=team-token-2024
# The client dials TLS by default; this server is plaintext until you enable TLS (below)
export CHATCLI_ALLOW_INSECURE=true

# Alias for quick access
alias cia='chatcli connect'
```

```bash
# Interactive mode
chatcli connect

# Quick one-shot
chatcli connect -p "Explain the Repository pattern in Go"
```

Devs who prefer to use their own credentials can do so:

```bash
# Dev who has a Claude Pro subscription
chatcli connect --use-local-auth

# Dev who prefers OpenAI
chatcli connect --provider OPENAI --llm-key sk-my-personal-key

# Dev who uses GitHub Copilot
chatcli connect --use-local-auth --provider COPILOT
```

The server accepts both modes simultaneously. Devs can use the server credentials (default) or provide their own.

For production environments, add TLS:

```bash
# Generate certificates (e.g., with Let's Encrypt or certbot)
# Or use mkcert for development

# Start with TLS
chatcli server \
  --tls-cert /etc/chatcli/cert.pem \
  --tls-key /etc/chatcli/key.pem \
  --token team-token-2024
```

Devs connect with:

```bash
chatcli connect server:50051 --tls --token team-token-2024
```

Configure the server with multiple API keys. Devs can choose the provider:

```bash
# Server with OpenAI, Claude, Google AI, and Copilot
export OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-xxx
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-xxx
export GOOGLEAI_API_KEY=AIzaSy-xxx
export GITHUB_COPILOT_TOKEN=ghu_xxx  # or use /auth login github-copilot
export LLM_PROVIDER=CLAUDEAI  # default
chatcli server
```

```bash
# Dev chooses the provider
chatcli connect --provider OPENAI
chatcli connect --provider GOOGLEAI
chatcli connect --provider COPILOT --use-local-auth
chatcli connect  # uses the default (CLAUDEAI)
```

Provision agents, skills, and plugins on the server so the entire team has access to the same resources:

```bash
helm install chatcli oci://ghcr.io/diillson/charts/chatcli \
  --namespace tools --create-namespace \
  --set llm.provider=CLAUDEAI \
  --set secrets.anthropicApiKey=sk-ant-xxx \
  --set server.token=team-token-2024 \
  --set agents.enabled=true \
  --set-file agents.definitions.devops-senior\\.md=agents/devops-senior.md \
  --set skills.enabled=true \
  --set-file skills.definitions.k8s-best-practices\\.md=skills/k8s-best-practices.md \
  --set plugins.enabled=true \
  --set plugins.initImage=myregistry/chatcli-plugins:latest
```

```bash
# Create ConfigMap with agents
kubectl create configmap chatcli-agents \
  --from-file=devops-senior.md=agents/devops-senior.md \
  --from-file=go-expert.md=agents/go-expert.md \
  -n tools

# Reference in Helm
helm install chatcli oci://ghcr.io/diillson/charts/chatcli \
  --set agents.enabled=true \
  --set agents.existingConfigMap=chatcli-agents
```

The server reads the agents only at startup, and Helm cannot hash a ConfigMap it does not render: after editing `chatcli-agents`, run `kubectl -n tools rollout restart deploy/chatcli`. Agents defined inline in `agents.definitions` roll the pods on `helm upgrade` by themselves.

In addition to provisioning skills via ConfigMaps, you can enable the **Skill Registry** to search and install skills from remote registries directly on the server:

```bash
helm install chatcli oci://ghcr.io/diillson/charts/chatcli \
  --set skillRegistry.enabled=true \
  --set skillRegistry.registryUrls="https://skills.mycompany.com/api/v1"
```

After connecting to the server, use the `/skill` commands to manage skills:

```bash
/skill search kubernetes     # Search across all registries
/skill install k8s-ops       # Install on the server
/agent skills                # Verify the skill is available
```

When devs connect, they automatically see the server resources:

```text
Connected to ChatCLI server (version: 1.3.0, provider: CLAUDEAI, model: claude-sonnet-4-6)
 Server has 2 plugins, 2 agents, 3 skills available
```

---

## Operational Tips

```bash
# Docker
docker logs chatcli-server -f

# Kubernetes
kubectl logs -f deployment/chatcli -n tools
```

```bash
# The Dockerfile includes a built-in health check
docker inspect chatcli-server --format='{{.State.Health.Status}}'
```

Set `LOG_LEVEL=info` on the server to log each request with the provider and model used.

```bash
# Docker Compose: ~/.chatcli lives in the chatcli-home volume (HOME=/home/nonroot)
docker cp chatcli-server:/home/nonroot/.chatcli/sessions ./backup/

# Kubernetes: PVC already configured in the Helm chart
```

When connected to a server, the `/session` commands interact with **both** local and remote:

- **`/session save`** asks where to save: local, remote, or both
- **`/session list`** displays sessions from both in separate sections
- **`/session load`** searches both; if found in both, asks which one to load
- **`/session delete`** checks both; if found in both, asks which one to remove

This allows devs to save sessions on the server to share with the team, or locally for offline use.

---

## Summary

| Component | Configuration |
|------------|-------------|
| Server | `chatcli server --token X` |
| Client | `chatcli connect --token X` |
| Env Vars | `CHATCLI_REMOTE_ADDR`, `CHATCLI_REMOTE_TOKEN` |
| TLS | `--tls-cert`, `--tls-key` (server) / `--tls` (client) |
| Credentials | Server (default) or client (`--llm-key` / `--use-local-auth`) |
| Agents/Skills | `agents.enabled=true` + ConfigMap (Helm) or `agents.configMapRef` (Operator) |
| Plugins | `plugins.enabled=true` + init container or PVC (Helm) or `plugins.image` (Operator) |



===== FILE: cookbook/scheduler-automations.mdx =====
TITLE: Scheduler automation recipes

# Scheduler automation recipes

Hands-on recipes for the [**Scheduler (Chronos)**](/features/scheduler). All assume the daemon is running (`chatcli daemon start --detach`) — but they work identically in in-process mode if you keep chatcli open.

---

## 1. Terraform deploy + K8s wait + validate

Apply infrastructure, wait for the deployment to become `Available`, then print the final state. One-liner — you can close the CLI.

```bash
❯ /schedule tf-apply --when +0s \
  --do "shell: cd infra && terraform apply -auto-approve" \
  --triggers verify-k8s

❯ /schedule verify-k8s --when manual \
  --do "/run kubectl get pods,svc,ingress -n prod" \
  --wait "k8s:deployment/prod/api:Available" \
  --timeout 20m

❯ /jobs tree
└─ ▶ tf-apply (a1b2c3d4)
   └─ ⛓ verify-k8s (e5f6g7h8)

❯ exit    # safe to close — daemon keeps running
```

Come back later:

```bash
❯ /jobs history
  completed  a1b2c3d4   tf-apply     — success (4m 12s)
  completed  e5f6g7h8   verify-k8s   — success (3m 40s)

❯ /jobs logs e5f6g7h8
  #1 2026-04-23T20:18:04Z  success (3m40s)
    k8s wait: deployment/prod/api Available=True (expected True)
  #2 2026-04-23T20:21:44Z  success (300ms)
    NAME           READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
    api-7d9...     1/1     Running   0          3m
```

---

## 2. Docker compose + healthcheck + smoke tests

```bash
❯ /schedule boot --when +0s \
  --do "shell: docker compose up -d" \
  --triggers smoke

❯ /schedule smoke --when manual \
  --do "/run pytest tests/smoke -v" \
  --wait "and(docker:api:healthy, http://localhost:8080/health==200)" \
  --timeout 5m \
  --on-timeout fallback
```

The `and(...)` condition only satisfies when the container is healthy **and** the endpoint returns 200 — avoids the classic "container up but app still initializing" case.

---

## 3. Nightly backup via cron

```bash
❯ /schedule nightly-backup --cron "0 2 * * *" \
  --do "shell: ./scripts/backup-db.sh" \
  --timeout 1h \
  --max-retries 3 \
  --ttl 168h \
  --tag env=prod --tag category=backup
```

- Runs every day at 02:00 local time.
- 1h timeout (long restore tolerated).
- Up to 3 retries with exponential backoff.
- TTL of 7 days in `/jobs history`.
- Filterable by tag in `/jobs list --tag category=backup`.

---

## 4. Wait and notify Slack

No scheduling — a wait that pings when the DB comes back:

```bash
❯ /wait --until tcp://db.prod:5432 \
  --then "POST https://hooks.slack.com/services/XXX | db is back" \
  --every 10s --timeout 30m --async
```

`--async` returns the command immediately (you get the job ID). When the port opens, the webhook fires.

---

## 5. DAG pipeline — multi-stage

Canary deploy → smoke → rollout → notify:

```bash
# Terminal stages set to "manual" — only fire via upstream Triggers
❯ /schedule canary --when manual --do "shell: kubectl apply -f canary.yaml" --triggers canary-smoke
❯ /schedule canary-smoke --when manual --do "/run pytest tests/smoke -v" \
  --wait "k8s:deployment/prod/api-canary:Available" --timeout 10m \
  --triggers rollout

❯ /schedule rollout --when manual --do "shell: kubectl apply -f prod.yaml" --triggers notify
❯ /schedule notify --when manual \
  --do "POST https://hooks.slack.com/services/XXX | deploy v2.1 successful"

# Fire the top — the cascade wires up automatically
❯ /schedule start --when +0s --do noop --triggers canary

❯ /jobs tree
└─ ✔ start (…)
   └─ ▶ canary (…)
      └─ ⛓ canary-smoke (…)
         └─ ⛓ rollout (…)
            └─ ⛓ notify (…)
```

If any stage fails, the failure cascade propagates — `rollout` and `notify` become `StatusFailed` with `dependency failed` in the message.

---

## 6. Agent scheduling itself (ReAct)

You ask for something long-running; the agent decides to pause and come back:

```
❯ /agent apply the terraform and then give me the cluster state

  [agent emits <tool_call name="@scheduler" .../>]
  agent: I scheduled a "tf-apply-check" job: runs terraform apply first,
  waits for the deployment to become Available, then runs kubectl get pods.
  I'll bring you the result when it finishes. Go work on other things.

❯ explain this folder's README for me
  [agent answers about the README; the job keeps running in parallel]

❯ ...10 minutes later...
  [on the next agent turn, the system injects into history:
   "Scheduler job tf-apply-check completed — output: NAME READY STATUS..."]
```

The agent uses `schedule` with `async:true` + `triggers` to chain; on the next conversation the result is already in context.

---

## 7. Self-limited job (budget)

Explicit rate-limit and budget to avoid LLM cost runaway:

```bash
❯ /schedule nightly-report --cron "0 7 * * 1" \
  --do "llm: Summarize the last 50 merged PRs this week" \
  --max-retries 1 \
  --timeout 2m
```

- One retry only.
- 2m cap on the LLM call (fast-fail if the model hangs).

Combine with `CHATCLI_SCHEDULER_RATE_LIMIT_OWNER_RPS` for a global cap.

---

## 8. Periodic probe with auto circuit breaker

```bash
❯ /schedule api-probe --every 30s \
  --do "/run if the /health failed, open a github issue" \
  --wait "http://api.prod.com/health==200" \
  --on-timeout fire_anyway \
  --max-polls 3
```

If the health check is down for 3 polls (90s), `--on-timeout fire_anyway` fires the action anyway — which in turn asks the agent to open the issue.

After 5 consecutive failures in 60s, the `http_status` circuit breaker opens for 30s — subsequent probes return `OutcomeBreakerOff` without hammering the downed API.

---

## 9. Cancel in bulk by tag

```bash
❯ /jobs list --tag env=staging
  ...20 staging jobs...

# Via external shell script:
for id in $(chatcli daemon status ...  # IPC JSON parsing (future)
            | jq -r '.jobs[] | select(.tags.env=="staging") | .id'); do
  chatcli /jobs cancel $id "staging cleanup"
done
```

Until the CLI exposes `/jobs cancel --tag` directly, the IPC + shell combination is enough.

---

## 10. Audit and troubleshooting

```bash
# See what ran
❯ /jobs history

# Detail (with all transitions + history)
❯ /jobs show a1b2c3d4

# Tail the execution logs
❯ /jobs logs a1b2c3d4

# Audit log — every mutation in JSONL
$ tail -f ~/.chatcli/scheduler/audit.log | jq .

# Prometheus metrics (gRPC server or operator)
$ curl localhost:8888/metrics | grep chatcli_scheduler
```

Each audit entry includes `type`, `timestamp`, `job_id`, `status`, `message`, and full event payload — use it as the basis for alerts in Splunk/Datadog/Loki.

---

## Common troubleshooting

Check:
- Does `/config scheduler` show `enabled`?
- Does `CHATCLI_SCHEDULER_ACTION_ALLOWLIST` include the action type?
- Did the rate limiter reject it? (see `chatcli_scheduler_enqueue_errors_total{reason="rate_limited"}`)
- Is the daemon running? `chatcli daemon status`.

- Did you set `--timeout`? (default 30m)
- Check breaker state: `/config scheduler` shows breakers in the daemon section; or metric `chatcli_scheduler_breaker_state`.
- Test the condition in isolation with `/wait --until <cond> --async` — when it fires, you know the evaluator works.

Stale socket from a previous crash. `start` tries to clean it automatically, but may fail if another process is still holding it:

```bash
$ chatcli daemon status        # "not running"?
$ rm /tmp/chatcli-scheduler.sock{,.pid}
$ chatcli daemon start --detach
```

Your shell command is classified by CoderMode at `/schedule` time (preflight). Two cases:

**ErrShellPolicyDeny** — the command (or a matching pattern) is on the denylist in `~/.chatcli/coder_policy.json`. `--i-know` does NOT clear this rejection; denylist is authoritative. Remove with:
```bash
/config security forget "@coder exec <pattern>"
```
Or edit the JSON directly if you prefer bulk edits.

**ErrShellPolicyAsk** — the command would hit an "Allow once / Allow always / Deny" prompt if run interactively. The scheduler never prompts. Three ways out:

1. Pre-authorize this specific job with `--i-know`:
   ```bash
   /schedule backup --when "+30s" --do "shell: my-tool --backup" --i-know
   ```
2. Permanently add to allowlist — one liner:
   ```bash
   /config security allow "@coder exec my-tool"
   ```
   You can also run the command once through interactive `/coder` and choose **"Allow always"** on the prompt (same `PolicyManager.AddRule` infrastructure underneath). Both persist to `~/.chatcli/coder_policy.json`.
3. Convert to `agent_task` or `slash_cmd` — commands run via agent go through interactive policy at fire time.

**Defense in depth:** the bridge re-checks at fire time. If you added a new Deny rule AFTER a cron job was scheduled, the next fire fails with `ErrShellPolicyDeny` in the log — it won't run.

For automation in an ephemeral CI container where you control the sandbox:

```bash
# 1. Operator enables bypass
export CHATCLI_SCHEDULER_SHELL_ALLOW_BYPASS=true

# 2. Job sets bypass_safety in the JSON spec
/schedule ci-prep --when +0s --do '{"type":"shell","payload":{"command":"any-command","bypass_safety":true}}'
```

Both must be present — `bypass_safety` alone without the operator's env var is rejected. This prevents an agent from writing `bypass_safety:true` in a spec and slipping past the policy without the operator having enabled it.

---

## Next steps

Full architecture, invariants and plug-in patterns.

All flags and subcommands.



===== FILE: cookbook/security-hardening-production.mdx =====
TITLE: Production Security Hardening

# Production Security Hardening

This guide hardens the two production shapes:

- **the ChatCLI server**, installed with the `chatcli` Helm chart or managed by the operator as an `Instance`;
- **the operator**, with its REST API and web dashboard.

It only uses controls that exist in the code. The [last section](#what-chatcli-does-not-protect) lists what ChatCLI does not protect, and what to put in place instead.

For the full AIOps install (operator, Instance, policies, observability), follow [AIOps Production Setup](/cookbook/aiops-production-setup). This page is about the security settings on top of it.

## Prerequisites

- Helm 3.8 or later, `kubectl`, `openssl`.
- A namespace for the server (`chatcli` below) and rights to create Secrets in it.
- For the certificate steps, cert-manager or your own CA.

## The server's security model

Read this before you choose settings. It explains what each control does and does not cover.

| Area | Behavior |
|---|---|
| Listener | gRPC on port 50051. Inside Kubernetes it binds every interface, and **refuses to start** without a credential (token, JWT material or client CA). JWT material that fails to load does not count: if it is the only credential, the server refuses to start. |
| TLS | Optional on the server, TLS 1.3 minimum when on. The operator always dials TLS, so an operator-managed Instance must enable it. |
| Shared token | Every holder is the same identity (`legacy-token`) with the **admin** role, and they all share one rate-limit bucket. |
| JWT | HS256 (`CHATCLI_JWT_SECRET`) or RS256 (`CHATCLI_JWT_PUBLIC_KEY`), never both. `exp` is required; `iss` and `aud` are checked when configured. The server verifies tokens but never issues them. |
| mTLS | `CHATCLI_SERVER_TLS_CLIENT_CA` makes **every** connection present a client certificate. A caller with only a certificate gets `CHATCLI_MTLS_ROLE` (default `user`). A bearer token, when present, wins over the certificate. |
| Roles | `admin`, `user` (JWT `operator` or `user`) and `readonly` (JWT `viewer` or `readonly`). Roles are enforced only on the pipeline execution RPCs (admin), some hub operations, plugin management and remote plugins (a read-only caller lists and downloads none). A read-only caller can still send prompts and manage its sessions, so treat every valid credential as permission to spend your LLM budget. |
| Rate limit | Per authenticated subject, 10 requests per second with a burst of 20 by default. It is always on. |
| Unauthenticated endpoints | `grpc.health.v1` and the `Health` RPC; the metrics port (`/metrics`, `/healthz`), plain HTTP on every interface. |

## Step 1: Choose and create the credential

    The simplest option, for machine clients you control. Everyone who has it is admin.

    ```bash
    kubectl create namespace chatcli
    kubectl -n chatcli create secret generic chatcli-token \
      --from-literal=token="$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
    ```

    Server chart: put `CHATCLI_SERVER_TOKEN` in the Secret named by `secrets.existingSecret`, or set `server.token`, which the chart stores in a Secret and never passes as a command-line argument. Instance: `spec.server.token: {name: chatcli-token, key: token}`.
  
    One shared secret verifies tokens you mint, each with its own subject, role and expiry.

    ```bash
    kubectl -n chatcli create secret generic chatcli-jwt \
      --from-literal=secret="$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
    ```

    Server chart: `security.jwtSecretRef: {name: chatcli-jwt, key: secret}`, plus `security.jwtIssuer` and `security.jwtAudience` (leave the inline `security.jwtSecret` empty: setting both fails the render). Instance: `spec.server.security.jwtSecretRef`, `jwtIssuer`, `jwtAudience`; the operator then mints its own short-lived tokens with the same secret.

    The server does not issue tokens. Mint them with your identity system, or, for a first test, with this script (standard library only):

    ```bash
    export JWT_SECRET="$(kubectl -n chatcli get secret chatcli-jwt -o jsonpath='{.data.secret}' | base64 -d)"
    python3 - <<'EOF'
    import base64, hashlib, hmac, json, os, time
    b64 = lambda d: base64.urlsafe_b64encode(d).rstrip(b"=")
    header = b64(json.dumps({"alg": "HS256", "typ": "JWT"}).encode())
    claims = b64(json.dumps({
        "sub": "alice@example.com",
        "role": "user",                  # admin | operator/user | viewer/readonly
        "iss": "chatcli-prod",           # must equal security.jwtIssuer
        "aud": "chatcli",                # must equal security.jwtAudience (a string, not a list)
        "exp": int(time.time()) + 3600,  # required
    }).encode())
    sig = b64(hmac.new(os.environ["JWT_SECRET"].encode(), header + b"." + claims, hashlib.sha256).digest())
    print((header + b"." + claims + b"." + sig).decode())
    EOF
    ```
  
    Your identity provider signs, and the server holds only the public key.

    ```bash
    kubectl -n chatcli create secret generic chatcli-jwt-pub \
      --from-file=public.pem=idp-public.pem
    ```

    Server chart: `security.jwtPublicKeyRef: {name: chatcli-jwt-pub, key: public.pem}` (not together with the inline `security.jwtPublicKey`: both fail the render). Instance: `spec.server.security.jwtPublicKeyRef`. The operator cannot mint RS256 tokens, so it needs its own credential: `spec.server.security.operatorTokenRef` (a token your IdP issued for it) or `operatorClientCertSecretName`.

    The `aud` claim is compared as a single string. Tokens whose `aud` is a list are rejected when `jwtAudience` is set.
  
    Every connection must present a certificate signed by your client CA. Use a CA dedicated to clients, not the CA that signed the server certificate, or every certificate that CA ever issued becomes a valid client.

    ```bash
    kubectl -n chatcli create secret generic chatcli-client-ca \
      --from-file=ca.crt=client-ca.crt
    ```

    Server chart: mount it with `extraVolumes`/`extraVolumeMounts` and set `security.tlsClientCA` to the mounted path (TLS must be on). Instance: `spec.server.tls.clientCASecretName: chatcli-client-ca`, plus `spec.server.security.operatorClientCertSecretName` for the operator's own certificate.

    Clients: `CHATCLI_TLS_CLIENT_CERT` and `CHATCLI_TLS_CLIENT_KEY` with `chatcli connect --tls`. There is no command-line flag for the client certificate.
  
## Step 2: TLS certificates

The certificate must cover the name clients dial. An operator-managed Instance is dialed as `<instance>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local`, which needs a private CA; see [TLS certificate](/cookbook/aiops-production-setup#tls-certificate) in the AIOps setup. For a server reached from outside the cluster under a public name:

```yaml
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: Certificate
metadata:
  name: chatcli-tls
  namespace: chatcli
spec:
  secretName: chatcli-tls
  issuerRef:
    name: letsencrypt-prod        # your ClusterIssuer
    kind: ClusterIssuer
  dnsNames:
    - chatcli.example.com
```

With the server chart, TLS needs two values. `tls.existingSecret` mounts the Secret at `/etc/chatcli/tls`, and the certificate and key paths default to `/etc/chatcli/tls/tls.crt` and `/etc/chatcli/tls/tls.key` (set `certFile` / `keyFile` only for other file names). `tls.enabled: true` without a Secret or both paths fails the install rather than serving plaintext:

```yaml
tls:
  enabled: true
  existingSecret: chatcli-tls
```

cert-manager renewals change the Secret, but the server reads the certificate only at startup. Restart the Deployment after a renewal, or use an operator-managed Instance, which rolls its pods when the TLS Secret changes.

## Step 3: Deploy the server with hardened values

Every key below exists in the chart's schema, which rejects unknown keys. This example uses HS256 JWTs.

```bash
kubectl -n chatcli create secret generic chatcli-llm-keys \
  --from-literal=ANTHROPIC_API_KEY='<anthropic-key>'
kubectl -n chatcli create secret generic chatcli-encryption-key \
  --from-literal=key="$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
```

```yaml
# values-prod.yaml
llm:
  provider: CLAUDEAI
  model: claude-sonnet-5

secrets:
  existingSecret: chatcli-llm-keys

server:
  port: 50051
  metricsPort: 9090             # /metrics and /healthz, plain HTTP: keep it behind the NetworkPolicy
  grpcReflection: false

tls:
  enabled: true
  existingSecret: chatcli-tls   # certFile/keyFile default to /etc/chatcli/tls/tls.crt and tls.key

security:
  jwtSecretRef:
    name: chatcli-jwt
    key: secret
  jwtIssuer: chatcli-prod
  jwtAudience: chatcli
  rateLimitRps: 20
  rateLimitBurst: 40
  auditLogPath: /var/log/chatcli/audit.jsonl
  agentSecurityMode: strict     # the default; set it so a stray env var cannot flip it
  envRedactMode: strict

extraEnv:
  - name: CHATCLI_ENCRYPTION_KEY
    valueFrom:
      secretKeyRef:
        name: chatcli-encryption-key
        key: key

extraVolumes:
  - name: audit
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: chatcli-audit   # create it first; the root filesystem is read-only
extraVolumeMounts:
  - name: audit
    mountPath: /var/log/chatcli

persistence:
  enabled: true
  size: 5Gi

rbac:
  create: false                  # no Secret access either way, but it grants workload and node remediation verbs; skip it when the watcher is off

networkPolicy:
  enabled: true
  ingressFrom:                   # applies to the gRPC and metrics ports
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: chatcli-clients
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: monitoring
  egress: restricted             # DNS, 443 and the Kubernetes API only
  kubernetesApiPort: 6443

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: 200m
    memory: 256Mi
  limits:
    cpu: "1"
    memory: 1Gi

serviceMonitor:
  enabled: true
```

```bash
helm install chatcli oci://ghcr.io/diillson/charts/chatcli \
  --version 1.211.2 \
  --namespace chatcli \
  -f values-prod.yaml
```

The chart's default pod settings already run as non-root (UID 1000) with a read-only root filesystem, no capabilities and the `RuntimeDefault` seccomp profile, and meet the `restricted` Pod Security Standard. Probes use `/healthz` on the metrics port and a TCP check on the gRPC port, which work with TLS on.

The chart renders a PodDisruptionBudget only when `replicaCount` is greater than 1. Several replicas on one `ReadWriteOnce` sessions volume fail to schedule across nodes. Use `persistence.accessModes: [ReadWriteMany]` with a storage class that supports it, or stay on one replica; `ReadWriteOncePod` with more than one replica fails the render. Without `ReadWriteMany`, the chart stops the old pod before starting the new one on each rollout (`maxSurge: 0`, `maxUnavailable: 1`), so an upgrade has a short gap instead of hanging on the volume; see [Rollouts on the sessions volume](/getting-started/docker-deployment#rollouts-on-the-sessions-volume).

The same controls on an operator-managed Instance:

```yaml
spec:
  server:
    token: {name: chatcli-token, key: token}   # or security.jwtSecretRef / jwtPublicKeyRef
    tls:
      enabled: true
      secretName: chatcli-prod-tls
      # clientCASecretName: chatcli-client-ca   # mTLS
    security:
      rateLimitRps: 20
      rateLimitBurst: 40
      # enableReflection stays unset (false)
  features:
    encryptionKeyRef: {name: chatcli-encryption-key, key: key}
  resources:                                    # the operator sets none
    requests: {cpu: 250m, memory: 512Mi}
    limits: {cpu: "1", memory: 1Gi}
```

## Step 4: Audit trail

With `CHATCLI_AUDIT_LOG_PATH` (chart `security.auditLogPath`, Instance `spec.server.security.auditLogPath`), the server appends one JSON line per RPC to a hash-chained file. Each line records the time, action and method, the caller (`user:<subject>` or `anonymous`), the role, the client host, the result (`success`, `error` or `denied`) and the duration. The path must be absolute and on a writable, persistent volume, as in step 3. On an Instance the only writable paths are `emptyDir` volumes, lost when the pod restarts, unless you enable persistence.

To check that nobody edited the file, read it from the volume (for example in a helper pod that mounts the same PVC) and run, in the `chatcli` REPL:

```text
/config security verify-audit /path/to/audit.jsonl
```

The operator does not write a file audit log. It records its actions as `AuditEvent` resources, readable with `kubectl get auditevents -A` and exportable through `GET /api/v1/audit/export`. Enable the Kubernetes API server audit log to capture who changed ChatCLI resources.

## Step 5: Encryption at rest

With `CHATCLI_ENCRYPTION_KEY` set, the server encrypts what it writes under `~/.chatcli` (sessions, history, memory and other local stores). Pass it from a Secret, as in step 3, or with `spec.features.encryptionKeyRef` on an Instance. Do not use the chart's `security.encryptionKey`: it puts the key in the pod spec as a plain value. Rotation is described in [Security](/features/security).

## Step 6: Agent, pipeline and plugin controls

These matter only if the server runs agent work or loads plugins.

- **Pipeline.** `pipeline.enabled` (Instance `spec.pipeline.enabled`) lets clients run the coder, agent and tools on the server. Those RPCs require the admin role. Leave it off unless you need it.
- **Command allowlist.** `strict` is the default mode: only commands in the allowlist run, and `CHATCLI_AGENT_ALLOWLIST` adds more (separated by `,` or `;`). The built-in list includes shells and interpreters such as `bash`, `python`, `node` and `eval`, so it is not a sandbox. Contain the pod instead: restricted NetworkPolicy egress, no chart RBAC when the watcher is off (`rbac.create: false`), and nothing sensitive on the pod's volumes.
- **Plugins.** At load, every plugin needs a detached signature (`<plugin>.sig`) that verifies against a public key in `~/.chatcli/trusted-keys/`. Unsigned plugins, and plugins on a machine with no trusted key, are skipped unless `CHATCLI_ALLOW_UNSIGNED_PLUGINS=true`, which you must never set in production. A plugin whose signature does not verify is always skipped.

  ```bash
  chatcli plugin keygen --output ./keys                    # plugin-signing.key and plugin-signing.pub
  chatcli plugin sign --binary ./my-plugin --key ./keys/plugin-signing.key
  chatcli plugin trust --key ./keys/plugin-signing.pub      # on each machine that loads it
  chatcli plugin verify --binary ./my-plugin
  ```

  In a pod, `~/.chatcli` is `/home/chatcli/.chatcli`. Mount the trusted public keys at `/home/chatcli/.chatcli/trusted-keys` with `extraVolumes`/`extraVolumeMounts`. Keep the private key out of the cluster.


## Step 7: Operator, REST API and dashboard

    The REST API and dashboard authenticate only with the `X-API-Key` header, against the Secret `chatcli-operator-secrets` (key `api-keys`) in the operator's namespace. Create one key per team and role (`viewer`, `operator`, `admin`), and keep admin keys few. See [Dashboard and REST API keys](/cookbook/aiops-production-setup#8-dashboard-and-rest-api-keys) for the commands.

    Keys are re-read every 30 seconds. To revoke one, remove it from the Secret. Deleting both the Secret and the ConfigMap `chatcli-operator-config` revokes every key within about 30 seconds. An edit that leaves invalid YAML does not revoke anything: the last valid key set stays in force and the operator logs the error. Give each approver their own key and set its `name`: approvals are recorded against the key, and a quorum counts each key once.
  
    `security.devMode: true` (`CHATCLI_OPERATOR_DEV_MODE`) makes the REST API accept every caller as admin while no keys are loaded. The variable is read with boolean semantics, case-insensitively: `true`, `TRUE`, `1` and `t` all turn it on, and the startup log reports the same mode the API applies. Keep it `false`.
  
    Terminate TLS at your ingress, or serve HTTPS (TLS 1.3) from the operator itself:

    ```yaml
    # values-operator.yaml
    extraVolumes:
      - name: api-tls
        secret:
          secretName: chatcli-operator-api-tls
    extraVolumeMounts:
      - name: api-tls
        mountPath: /etc/chatcli-operator/api-tls
        readOnly: true
    security:
      apiTLS:
        certFile: /etc/chatcli-operator/api-tls/tls.crt
        keyFile: /etc/chatcli-operator/api-tls/tls.key
    ```
  
    Cross-origin calls are denied until you name an origin in `security.corsAllowedOrigins`. The dashboard is served from the same origin as the API and needs none.
  
    Set `networkPolicy.enabled: true` on the operator chart, restrict `apiIngressFrom` and `metricsIngressFrom`, and keep `egress: restricted`. The values are in [step 1 of the AIOps setup](/cookbook/aiops-production-setup#1-install-the-operator).
  
    The operator runs with a cluster-wide ClusterRole that can read and write Secrets in every namespace, change workloads, create pods (chaos stress pods), evict pods (`DrainNode`) and update nodes. No admission webhook checks what users submit, so Kubernetes RBAC is the only gate on who can make the operator act. Grant `create`/`update` on `remediationplans`, `runbooks`, `instances`, `chaosexperiments`, `approvalrequests` and the policy kinds only to the people who should hold that power. The chart pre-provisions the ClusterRoles `chatcli-role-viewer`, `chatcli-role-operator`, `chatcli-role-admin` and `chatcli-role-superadmin`; bind them yourself, since nothing binds them automatically.
  
    - `security.allowedDiagnosticCommands` adds commands to the read-only `ExecDiagnostic` allowlist; everything else is refused.
    - `security.allowedResourceTypes` adds kinds that the `ApplyManifest` action may create. It extends the built-in list and never narrows it.
    - `security.logScrubPatterns` adds comma-separated regular expressions to the scrubbing applied to the logs and context the operator sends to the LLM. It does not scrub the operator's own logs.
    - Gate risky actions with an ApprovalPolicy: see [Approvals](/cookbook/aiops-production-setup#9-approvals).
  
## Step 8: Verify the images

Release images are signed with cosign (keyless) and carry SBOM and provenance attestations.

```bash
cosign verify ghcr.io/diillson/chatcli:1.211.2 \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
  --certificate-identity-regexp '^https://github\.com/diillson/chatcli/\.github/workflows/(3-publish-release|image-refresh)\.yml@refs/heads/main$'
```

Use the same command for `ghcr.io/diillson/chatcli-operator:1.211.2`. Pin image tags, or digests, in production instead of `latest`.

## Step 9: Verify the hardening

    ```bash
    kubectl -n chatcli port-forward svc/chatcli 50051:50051 &
    openssl s_client -connect localhost:50051 -servername chatcli.example.com -tls1_3 </dev/null 2>/dev/null \
      | openssl x509 -noout -subject -ext subjectAltName
    openssl s_client -connect localhost:50051 -tls1_2 </dev/null   # handshake fails
    ```
  
    ```bash
    kubectl -n chatcli exec deploy/chatcli -- \
      grpc-health-probe -addr=localhost:50051 -tls -tls-no-verify   # status: SERVING
    ```
    With mTLS, add `-tls-client-cert` and `-tls-client-key`.
  
    ```bash
    chatcli connect --addr localhost:50051 --tls --ca-cert ca.crt -p "hello"
    # fails: authentication failed
    chatcli connect --addr localhost:50051 --tls --ca-cert ca.crt --token "$JWT" -p "hello"
    # answers
    ```
    `ca.crt` is the CA of the server certificate (not needed for a public CA). `$JWT` is a token from step 1.
  
    ```bash
    grpcurl -cacert ca.crt localhost:50051 list
    # Failed to list services: server does not support the reflection API
    ```
  
    ```bash
    kubectl -n chatcli get pod -l app.kubernetes.io/name=chatcli \
      -o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.securityContext}{"\n"}{.items[0].spec.containers[0].securityContext}{"\n"}'
    ```
  
    ```bash
    kubectl -n chatcli get networkpolicy
    # From a namespace that is not allowed, both ports must time out:
    kubectl -n default run np-test --rm -it --restart=Never --image=curlimages/curl -- \
      curl -m 5 http://chatcli.chatcli.svc:9090/metrics
    ```
  
    After a few calls, the audit file on the volume grows by one line per RPC, and `/config security verify-audit` reports the chain as intact.
  
    ```bash
    kubectl -n chatcli-system port-forward svc/chatcli-operator 8090:8090 &
    curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://localhost:8090/api/v1/incidents   # 401
    ```
  
## What ChatCLI does not protect

These gaps exist in current releases. Cover each one with the mitigation next to it.

| Gap | What it means | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| No admission webhook | Nothing validates ChatCLI resources before they are stored. Anyone who can create a RemediationPlan, Runbook, Instance or ChaosExperiment can make the operator act with its ClusterRole | Kubernetes RBAC on the `platform.chatcli.io` resources; ApprovalPolicies for remediation |
| Metrics endpoints without authentication | The server's metrics port (`/metrics`, `/healthz`) listens on every interface in plain HTTP, and so does the operator's port 8080 | NetworkPolicy allowing only Prometheus (and the node CIDR for probes, where your CNI needs it) |
| No SSO or OIDC for the dashboard | Access is by API key only. The browser keeps the key in `localStorage`. Dashboard approvals are recorded as the typed name plus the key's identity: the name is free text, only the key is authenticated | Few keys, one per team and role, and one per approver where quorum rules apply (set each key's `name`); TLS; restrict ingress sources; put an authenticating proxy in front if you need per-person login |
| Coarse server roles | A `viewer`/`readonly` credential can still send prompts and manage its sessions | Issue credentials only to people allowed to use the LLM; run separate servers per trust boundary |
| Shared token is admin | Every holder has the same admin identity and shares one rate-limit bucket | JWT or mTLS for people; the token only for machine clients |
| No operator file audit log | The operator's own actions are only in `AuditEvent` resources | Kubernetes API audit logging; export `AuditEvent`s through the REST API |
| No automatic user RBAC | The `chatcli-role-*` ClusterRoles are never bound for you | Bind them to your groups |
| No NetworkPolicy, PDB or HPA for Instances | The operator creates none of them | Create them yourself (see the AIOps setup) |
| Optional Secret references on Instances | A missing Secret or key silently drops the variable; with the token gone the pod exits on startup | Check the Instance conditions and pod status after every change |
| Server log destinations | In a pod the log goes to stderr (`kubectl logs`) and to the pod's ephemeral file; the file is lost with the pod | Ship `kubectl logs` through your log collector; the audit trail is the durable record |
| Authentication failure limiter | Each client host may fail authentication 5 times in a burst, then once every 12 seconds; a host that exhausts it is refused (`authentication failed`, log `auth failure rate limit exceeded`) until a slot refills. Valid credentials never spend from it | Nothing to tune; find the caller with the wrong or expired credential |

## Final checklist

| Item | Done |
|---|---|
| Credential chosen: JWT or mTLS for people, shared token only for machines | |
| JWT `iss` and `aud` configured, tokens short-lived | |
| TLS on, certificate covering the dialed name, renewal handled | |
| mTLS client CA separate from the server CA (if used) | |
| Reflection off (`grpcReflection` / `enableReflection` false) | |
| Audit log on a persistent volume, chain verified | |
| Encryption key from a Secret, not a plain value | |
| Pipeline off unless needed; plugins signed and trusted keys mounted | |
| Server NetworkPolicy with restricted ingress and egress | |
| Server chart RBAC off when the watcher is off | |
| Operator dev mode off, API keys per team, revocation by editing the Secret | |
| REST API behind TLS, CORS closed | |
| Operator NetworkPolicy on | |
| RBAC on `platform.chatcli.io` resources limited to the right people | |
| Images verified with cosign and pinned | |

## Next steps

    Every security control in detail.
  
    The complete operator and Instance runbook.
  
    Instance fields and what the operator creates.
  
    Every variable the server and the operator read.
  


