TITLE: List Audit Events

# List Audit Events

  Filter by the namespace of the AuditEvent. Omit it to list every namespace.

  Filter by `eventType` (exact match), e.g. `issue_created`, `issue_resolved`, `issue_escalated`, `issue_contained`, `remediation_started`, `remediation_completed`, `remediation_failed`, `approval_requested`, `approval_approved`, `approval_rejected`, `approval_expired`, `notification_sent`, `slo_violation`, `sla_breach`

  Filter by severity: `info`, `warning`, `critical`

  Filter by the affected resource **name** (exact match on `resourceName`)

  Only events at or after this RFC 3339 timestamp (`spec.timestamp`, falling back to the creation time)

  Only events at or before this RFC 3339 timestamp

  Page number

  Items per page (maximum 100)

Minimum role: `viewer`. AuditEvents are written by the operator's controllers (Issue, Remediation, Notification, SLO and SLA reconcilers). The actor is the controller, except for `approval_approved` and `approval_rejected` after a human decision: then `actorType` is `user` and `actorName` lists the approvers recorded in the ApprovalRequest `status.decisions` (also in the `approvers` detail). An auto-approval or an expiry names `ApprovalReconciler`. Calls to this REST API (acknowledge, approve, runbook edits and so on) are **not** recorded as AuditEvents; the operator only logs them as `[REST]` lines in its own log.

`detail` flattens `spec.details` into `key=value` pairs joined by `; ` (map order, so the pair order is not stable). Items are sorted newest first by `timestamp` (the creation time when it is missing), so each page is a stable slice of the trail.

