## History Compaction and Payload Recovery

Controls the `HistoryCompactor` (3-level pipeline: trim → summarize → emergency) and the reactive detection of corporate-proxy/gateway limits. The budget check weighs the **whole payload** — message text plus native tool-call arguments, image payloads and system-part excess — so tool- or vision-heavy histories trigger compaction before hitting a proxy cap. See [Context Recovery](/features/context-recovery).

| Variable | Description | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `CHATCLI_CONTEXT_WINDOW` | **Global context-window override** (in tokens), valid for any provider/model. Takes precedence over the model catalog and the per-provider fallbacks. It is the escape hatch for gateways/agents whose real window differs from the catalog — e.g. a StackSpot agent backed by a larger or smaller foundation model. Since the compaction budget derives from the context window, this env directly controls when auto-compact fires. Accepts a positive integer only; invalid values are silently ignored. | _(auto from catalog)_ |
| `CHATCLI_PROMPT_CACHE_TTL` | Prompt-cache lifetime: `5m`, `1h` or `auto` (`auto` = `5m` on every surface until the session watches its own prefix expire during a pause the hour would have covered, then `1h`; long tool calls are covered by keep-alive refreshes instead). On Anthropic's direct API (API key, token and OAuth) it sets the marker TTL; with explicit Gemini caching it is the resource lifetime. The hour keeps the cached prefix warm through longer idle gaps at a higher write rate (2× input instead of 1.25×), so it pays off for sessions that pause between turns and costs more for rapid-fire ones. Applies to every marker in the request (system, tools and the rolling conversation breakpoint); Bedrock keeps the wire default. See [Cost tracking → Prompt caching](/features/cost-tracking#prompt-caching-by-provider). | `5m` |
| `CHATCLI_PROMPT_CACHE_KEEPALIVE` | Refreshes the 5-minute prompt-cache entry by re-sending the previous request with the least output the wire accepts, 4.5 minutes after each request, up to twelve times per stretch. `auto` refreshes while a run's tool call outlives the entry on every adapter that can (Anthropic API, OpenAI Chat Completions, Bedrock Mantle) and while idle on models whose cache reads cost at most 5% of the input price (Claude Fable 5.1); `on` also refreshes while idle on every adapter that can; `off` never. Refreshes are booked in `/cost`; the gateway never refreshes. See [Prefix curation](/features/prefix-curation). | `auto` |
| `CHATCLI_PROMPT_CACHE_EXPLICIT` | `true` lets adapters create provider cache **resources** — today Gemini `cachedContents` holding the system instruction — which bill storage per token-hour on top of the discounted reads. Conservative policy (4K+ tokens, two consecutive sightings, inline retry on rejection, release at shutdown); storage shows in `/cost`. See [Cost tracking → Explicit cache resources](/features/cost-tracking#explicit-cache-resources-gemini). | `false` |
| `CHATCLI_MEMORY_PROVIDER` | `builtin` or `mcp:<server>`: an MCP server exposing `memory_recall` / `memory_store` augments the auto-recall block and receives every turn (see [Memory → External memory provider](/features/bootstrap-memory#external-memory-provider-mcp)). | `builtin` |
| `CHATCLI_CONTEXT_ENGINE` | `builtin`, `mcp:<server>` (an MCP server exposing `context_compact` produces the compaction summary, with fallback to the embedded summarizer) or `provider` (adds the model's server-side context editing — Anthropic `clear_tool_uses` — on top of the local pipeline). See [Context recovery → External context engine](/features/context-recovery#external-context-engine-mcp). | `builtin` |
| `CHATCLI_COMPACT_MODEL` | `PROVIDER:model` that serves the Level 2 structured summary during compaction (e.g. `CLAUDEAI:claude-haiku-4-5-20251001`, `OPENAI:gpt-5.6-luna`). A cheaper, faster model here cuts the cost and the 30–90 s stall of compacting on a frontier model; the session client keeps serving the conversation. The same model serves the background memory worker (extraction, rollups, memory compaction), which can be a third of a session's bill on a frontier model. Unresolvable values are logged once and fall back to the session client. | _(session client)_ |
| `CHATCLI_MAX_PAYLOAD` | **Human-friendly ceiling** for POST body size. Accepts `5MB`, `512KB`, `2.5MB`, `5` (= 5 MB). When set, the compactor respects this limit as an extra cap on top of the model's context window, and pre-flight forces aggressive compaction on crossing 85% of it. **Essential behind corporate proxies** (Cloudflare, Akamai, WAF) that cap body size at 1-5 MB regardless of model context. | _(unset — no cap)_ |
| `CHATCLI_MAX_RECOVERY_ATTEMPTS` | Maximum context-overflow recovery attempts per session. | `3` |
| `CHATCLI_MAX_TOKEN_ESCALATIONS` | Maximum `max_tokens` escalations per session. | `2` |
| `CHATCLI_EMERGENCY_KEEP_MESSAGES` | Messages kept during Level 2 emergency truncation. | `10` |
| `CHATCLI_MICROCOMPACT_TRUNCATE_TURNS` | Age (in turns) at which old tool results start being truncated to head+tail preview — no LLM call. First line of defense before `NeedsCompaction` fires. | `2` |
| `CHATCLI_MICROCOMPACT_SUMMARIZE_TURNS` | Age (in turns) at which old tool results get replaced by a one-line summary (`[Old tool result cleared — N lines, N chars, type]`). | `4` |

Behind a corporate proxy you usually don't need to guess anymore: on the first rejection, ChatCLI **learns the cap from the exact size the gateway refused** (¾ of the rejected request) and applies it for the session. Set `CHATCLI_MAX_PAYLOAD` explicitly only when you know the proxy limit up front — an explicit value below the learned one always wins. ChatCLI leaves a 30% headroom for JSON overhead + system prompt + tool definitions.

When a 413/WAF/EOF hits, Bedrock errors carry the exact rejected request size and the session cap is derived from it (adaptive learned cap). Only when the size is unknown does ChatCLI fall back to assuming **4 MB** for the rest of the session. Either way it injects a one-time hint into history telling the AI to prefer line-ranged reads. If the system prompt alone (personas, skills, MCP tool docs) meets the rejected size, recovery fails fast with an actionable diagnosis instead of retrying — see [Context Recovery](/features/context-recovery).

