## MCP (Model Context Protocol)

| Variable | Description | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `CHATCLI_MCP_ENABLED` | Enable MCP manager | `false` |
| `CHATCLI_MCP_CONFIG` | Path to MCP configuration JSON | `~/.chatcli/mcp_servers.json` |
| `CHATCLI_MCP_TOOLS` | Exposure policy for `chatcli mcp-server`: `all` (default), `safe` (read-only capability only) or a comma-separated allowlist. See [MCP Server](/features/mcp-server). | `all` |
| `CHATCLI_MCP_DYNAMIC_TOOLS` | Re-run `tools/list` when a server emits `notifications/tools/list_changed`, reconciling the live tool registry (dynamic servers like HTTP Toolkit that expose tools only after a bootstrap call). Accepts `0`/`false`/`off`/`no` to disable — the notification then only lands in the channel inbox. See [Dynamic tool discovery](/features/mcp-integration#dynamic-tool-discovery). | `true` |
| `CHATCLI_MCP_DANGER` | `chatcli mcp-server` unattended dangerous-command policy: `allow` auto-approves like the gateway daemon; `block` declines dangerous shell commands in-band so the model can replan. See [MCP Server](/features/mcp-server). | `allow` |
| `CHATCLI_MCP_ELICITATION` | Approval forms via `elicitation/create` for clients that declare the capability. `off` disables the bridge and restores the legacy unattended contract — the escape hatch for clients that declare elicitation but never render dialogs. See [MCP Server](/features/mcp-server#approval-prompts-via-elicitation). | `on` |
| `CHATCLI_MCP_PERMISSION_TIMEOUT` | Max wait for one permission dialog — MCP elicitation forms **and** ACP `session/request_permission` (Go duration or plain seconds). On expiry the action denies fail-safe and the model is told the request went unanswered; later prompts in the same run fail fast. `0`/`off` lifts the bound to a **24h ceiling** — effectively unlimited for a human decision, but a dead-but-connected client can no longer pin the turn forever. MCP clients with a shorter `tools/call` timeout kill the call first — tune below your client's limit. | `600s` |
| `CHATCLI_MCP_RESOURCES` | Serve ChatCLI's local state (memory, contexts, knowledge, skills, sessions) as read-only `chatcli://` MCP resources. `off` disables the surface. | `on` |
| `CHATCLI_MCP_MAX_HISTORY` | Hard message cap for `ask_chatcli` session histories. `0` leaves the full pipeline bounded by token-aware compaction (the plain path keeps its legacy 30-message cap). | `0` |
| `CHATCLI_MCP_SESSION_AUTOSAVE` | Persist each live MCP session as a rolling `mcp-<session>` mirror after every turn; explicit value wins, unset follows `CHATCLI_SESSION_AUTOSAVE`. | `true` |
| `CHATCLI_MCP_HUB` | Join the [conversation hub](/features/conversation-hub) in resume mode, adopting the REPL/gateway thread for cross-channel continuity. `off` opts out. | `on` |
| `CHATCLI_MCP_HUB_PRINCIPAL` | Isolate the MCP server's hub thread under its own principal instead of sharing the default conversation. | *(hub default)* |

The MCP subsystem also manages `~/.chatcli/mcp/` for durable state: `channels.jsonl` (durable push-notification ring, with rotation) and `triggers.json` (opt-in trigger engine rules). These paths are fixed — no environment variables override them. Details: [MCP Channels](/features/mcp-channels) and [MCP Config](/reference/mcp-config).

